  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x^2 + 0.5 z^2)/2, drawn at half its value as height. The steep axis runs left to right. Each step is -rate × (slope + noise). Without noise, swings along x grow once the rate is above 2/2.4 ≈ 0.83.",
    "The noise is Gaussian in both directions and centred on the true slope, with spread sqrt((64-b)/(63b)): 1 at batch 1 and exactly 0 at 64. With noise, a single step can raise the loss. It lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Both arrows are steps drawn at 5 times their true length, on one scale. The purple arrow is the step the ball takes next. The dark arrow is the step the full 64 points would give. The step-length readout shows the purple step's true length.",
    "Diverged means the loss has gone above 5. Finished means 40 steps were taken. A ball or path point past the bowl's edge is drawn at the edge, but the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The trail holds the start point plus one point per step, so up to 41 points. The ghost keeps the last run. The noise sequence continues across new runs, and Reset replays it from the start."
  ]
}